hct advisor  /repo (config saved): 7 recommended, 4 suggestions

the repository
  recommended  audit/acceptance-expired  [security]
      Review the accepted vulnerabilities past their date in .hecatron/config.json audit.ignore: GHSA-1 (until 2026-01-01)
      Why: An acceptance's date is when it was to be looked at again; past it, the risk is accepted by no one.
      See: https://github.com/dnf/hecatron/blob/main/docs/agents.md#project-configuration
  recommended  audit/failed  [security]  (go)
      Fix or accept the known vulnerabilities hct audit go reported on 2026-10-27 (log .logs/audit/go.log)
      Why: A dependency with a known vulnerability is the easiest way in; accepting one with a reason and a date keeps the decision reviewed.
      See: https://github.com/dnf/hecatron/blob/main/docs/agents.md#project-configuration
  recommended  repo/branch-pinned-action  [security]
      Pin each GitHub Action to a release tag or a commit, not a branch (a uses: line names @main, @master or another branch)
      Why: A branch moves: whatever its owner pushes next runs in your CI, with your workflow's permissions and secrets.
      See: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use#using-third-party-actions
  suggestion   node/npm-ci  [performance]  (typescript)
      Install with npm ci in CI, not npm install
      Why: npm ci installs exactly what package-lock.json records, fails when it no longer matches package.json, and is faster on a clean machine.
      See: https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/commands/npm-ci
  suggestion   python/ci-covers  [ci]  (python)
      Run Python's tests in CI: no CI configuration mentions it
      Why: Tests that run only on someone's machine stop being run; CI runs them on every change.
      See: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/tutorials/build-and-test-code/python

go/
  recommended  go/toolchain-end-of-life  [currency]  (go)
      Move off Go 1.20 (pinned in go/go.mod): that line is past its end of life; the newest is 1.27
      Why: Each Go release is supported until there are two newer major releases; after that it gets no security fixes.
      See: https://go.dev/doc/devel/release#policy

python/
  recommended  python/pyproject  [currency]  (python)
      Add a pyproject.toml declaring the build system ([build-system]), and move the metadata from setup.py into it
      Why: pyproject.toml is the standard place for a project's build system and metadata; without it pip falls back to setuptools' deprecated legacy path.
      See: https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/guides/modernize-setup-py-project/
  recommended  python/toolchain-end-of-life  [currency]  (python)
      Move off Python 3.8 (pinned in python/.python-version): that line is past its end of life; the newest is 3.15
      Why: Each Python release line gets fixes for five years, then none, security fixes included.
      See: https://devguide.python.org/versions/

rust/
  suggestion   rust/toolchain-behind  [currency]  (rust)
      Update Rust 1.80 (pinned in rust/rust-toolchain.toml): it is 19 releases behind 1.99
      Why: Rust supports only its latest stable release, which every six weeks brings fixes, including security fixes; a toolchain a year behind has none of them.
      See: https://www.rust-lang.org/policies/security

typescript/
  recommended  node/lock-matches-manifest  [reproducibility]  (typescript)
      Update typescript/package-lock.json: it doesn't record the dependencies package.json declares
      Why: npm ci refuses to install when package-lock.json and package.json disagree; npm install updates the lock.
      See: https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/commands/npm-ci
  suggestion   node/toolchain-pinned  [reproducibility]  (typescript)
      Pin Node.js's version in .nvmrc (it runs 24.22.1 now, hecatron's default)
      Why: Node.js has no version setting of its own; nvm, fnm, volta and hecatron read .nvmrc, so everyone runs the release line the project is tested on.
      See: https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm#nvmrc
      Fix: hct advisor --fix creates typescript/.nvmrc

